# AcerHelper — hand the "wheel" group write access to the ryzen_smu driver's SMU mailbox attributes, ONCE PER
# BOOT, after the module is up. Installed by the app's "Grant hardware access" action to
# /etc/systemd/system/acer-helper-smu-perms.service (Infrastructure/HardwareAccess.cs).
#
# WHY THIS UNIT EXISTS — it is the deterministic half of a grant that had only a racy one. The udev rule that used
# to be the whole of it (packaging/60-acer-helper.rules, the ACTION=="bind" ryzen_smu block) CANNOT FIRE AT BOOT
# on a machine that loads this driver from the INITRAMFS, and that is measured on the machine this app was built
# for: dracut's systemd-modules-load inserts the module before the real systemd-udevd exists (journal:
# "systemd-modules-load[335]: Inserted module 'ryzen_smu'" during the initramfs run), so the bind uevent is
# emitted onto a bus nobody is listening to, and systemd-udev-trigger.service's coldplug replays add/change
# events — never a bind. The four attributes are absent from /etc/tmpfiles.d/acer-helper.conf for the same kind
# of reason (they do not exist yet at the tmpfiles pass), so NOTHING re-applied the grant and it was simply LOST
# at the next boot: the nodes came up -rw-r--r-- root root, the app's undervolt port gates on writing them
# (Infrastructure/Vendors/Generic/RyzenCurveOptimizer.Linux.cs), and the owner's Tuning tab disappeared with them.
# This unit runs after the module, on every boot, unconditionally — which is what the udev rule could not promise.
#
# WHAT WOULD MAKE IT DELETABLE: a distribution packaging of ryzen_smu that ships its own udev rule for these
# attribute files, or a kernel that applies a group to them itself — or an /etc/tmpfiles.d line that systemd
# guarantees to run after systemd-modules-load.service (today's acer-helper.conf entry runs in the tmpfiles pass,
# which is exactly the ordering that failed). Until one of those exists, deleting this file leaves the grant
# depending on a MANUAL module reload.
#
# THE FOUR NAMES ARE THE DRIVER'S WHOLE WRITABLE SURFACE, granted together for the reason the udev rule gives: the
# app drives "smn" alone (the command nodes cannot report a refusal), but a half-granted driver is a worse outcome
# for the other tools that share it. The paths are absolute because the driver's tree /sys/kernel/ryzen_smu_drv is
# a BARE KOBJECT — not a device, no uevent — so nothing can reach it by matching.
#
# THE DOUBLED DOLLAR SIGN IS NOT A TYPO: systemd expands $VAR in ExecStart from the service's own environment, and
# an undefined name becomes EMPTY — a bare "$f" would silently chgrp the directory instead of the four files. "$$"
# is systemd's escape for a literal "$", so the shell receives the loop variable it needs, exactly as "$$f" is what
# udev needs in the rule for the same reason.

[Unit]
Description=Grant the wheel group write access to the ryzen_smu SMU mailbox attributes
After=systemd-modules-load.service
ConditionPathExists=/sys/kernel/ryzen_smu_drv

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'for f in smu_args mp1_smu_cmd rsmu_cmd smn; do chgrp wheel /sys/kernel/ryzen_smu_drv/$$f 2>/dev/null && chmod g+w /sys/kernel/ryzen_smu_drv/$$f 2>/dev/null; done'

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
